xed{\\dfrac{g(n+1)}{f(n+1)} \\geqslant \\dfrac{g(n)}{f(n)}}\\)\n2. \\(\\boxed{\\dfrac{\\lambda}{\\lambda - 1}}\\)", "ground_truth_solution": ": \n**1. Given Information:**\n\n- \\( f(1) = f(2) = 1 \\)\n- \\( g(1) = g(2) = 2 \\)\n- \\( \\frac{f(n+1)}{f(n)} = \\lambda \\frac{f(n)}{f(n-1)} \\)\n- \\( \\frac{g(n+1)}{g(n)} \\geq \\lambda \\frac{g(n)}{g(n-1)} \\)\n- \\(\\lambda\\) is a non-zero parameter\n- \\( n = 2, 3, 4, \\ldots \\)\n\n#### Part 1: Prove: \\( \\frac{g(n+1)}{f(n+1)} \\geq \\frac{g(n)}{f(n)} \\) for \\( n \\in \\mathbb{N}^{+} \\)\n\n1. **Base cases:**\n   - For \\( n = 1 \\) and \\( n = 2 \\):\n     - \\( f(1) = f(2) = 1 \\)\n     - \\( g(1) = g(2) = 2 \\)\n\n2. **Using the given ratio equations:**\n   - Given \\( \\frac{f(n+1)}{f(n)} = \\lambda \\frac{f(n)}{f(n-1)} \\)\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{f(n+1)}{f(n)} = \\frac{f(2)}{f(1)} \\lambda^{n-1} = \\lambda^{n-1} \n   \\]\n\n3. **Similarly, for \\( g(n) \\):**\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{g(n+1)}{g(n)} \\geq \\frac{g(2)}{g(1)} \\lambda^{n-1} = \\lambda^{n-1} \n   \\]\n\n4. **Comparing the ratios:**\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{g(n+1)}{g(n)} \\geq \\frac{f(n+1)}{f(n)} \\text{ for any } n \\in \\mathbb{N}^{+}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Final step:**\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{g(n+1)}{f(n+1)} = \\frac{g(n+1)}{g(n)} \\times \\frac{g(n)}{f(n)} \\times \\left( \\frac{f(n)}{f(n+1)} \\right) \\geq \\frac{g(n)}{f(n)}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus,\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{ \\frac{g(n+1)}{f(n+1)} \\geq \\frac{g(n)}{f(n)} }\n   \\]\n\n#### Part 2: Prove: \\( \\frac{g(1)-f(1)}{g(2)-f(2)} + \\frac{g(2)-f(2)}{g(3)-f(3)} + \\cdots + \\frac{g(n)-f(n)}{g(n+1)-f(n+1)} < \\frac{\\lambda}{\\lambda-1} \\) for \\( \\lambda > 1 \\)\n\n1. **From the inequality \\( \\frac{g(n+1)}{f(n+1)} \\geq \\frac{g(n)}{f(n)} \\):**\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{g(n+1) - f(n+1)}{f(n+1)} \\geq \\frac{g(n) - f(n)}{f(n)}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Since \\( \\frac{g(n+1)}{f(n+1)} \\geq 2 \\):**\n\n   \\[\n   2 f(n+1) \\leq g(n+1)\n   \\]\n\n   - This implies \\( g(n+1) - f(n+1) > 0 \\):\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{g(n) - f(n)}{g(n+1) - f(n+1)} \\leq \\frac{f(n)}{f(n+1)} = \\frac{1}{\\lambda^{n-1}}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Sum of the series:**\n\n   The series can be evaluated as follows:\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{g(1)-f(1)}{g(2)-f(2)} + \\frac{g(2)-f(2)}{g(3)-f(3)} + \\cdots + \\frac{g(n)-f(n)}{g(n+1)-f(n+1)} \n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   \\leq 1 + \\frac{1}{\\lambda} + \\frac{1}{\\lambda^2} + \\cdots + \\frac{1}{\\lambda^{n-1}}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Sum of geometric progression:**\n\n   - Using the geometric series sum formula for \\( \\lambda > 1 \\):\n\n   \\[\n   S = 1 + \\frac{1}{\\lambda} + \\frac{1}{\\lambda^2} + \\cdots + \\frac{1}{\\lambda^{n-1}} = \\frac{1 - \\frac{1}{\\lambda^n}}{1 - \\frac{1}{\\lambda}} = \\frac{\\lambda}{\\lambda - 1} \\left( 1 - \\frac{1}{\\lambda^n} \\right)\n   \\]\n\n5. **Final inequality:**\n\n   Since \\( \\frac{1}{\\lambda^n} \\to 0 \\) as \\( n \\to \\infty \\),\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{g(1)-f(1)}{g(2)-f(2)} + \\frac{g(2)-f(2)}{g(3)-f(3)} + \\cdots + \\frac{g(n)-f(n)}{g(n+1)-f(n+1)} < \\frac{\\lambda}{\\lambda - 1} \n   \\]\n\n   Thus,\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{ \\frac{g(1)-f(1)}{g(2)-f(2)} + \\frac{g(2)-f(2)}{g(3)-f(3)} + \\cdots + \\frac{g(n)-f(n)}{g(n+1)-f(n+1)} < \\frac{\\lambda}{\\lambda - 1} }\n   \\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Find all integer triples \\((x, y, z)\\) such that \\(x^3 + y^3 + z^3 - 3xyz = 2003\\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to find all integer triples (x, y, z) such that x\u00b3 + y\u00b3 + z\u00b3 - 3xyz = 2003. Hmm, I remember that the left side of the equation looks familiar. Let me think... Oh, right! There's a formula for x\u00b3 + y\u00b3 + z\u00b3 - 3xyz. It factors into (x + y + z)(x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 + z\u00b2 - xy - yz - zx). So, the equation becomes (x + y + z)(x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 + z\u00b2 - xy - yz - zx) = 2003.\n\nNow, 2003 is a prime number, right? Let me check that. Well, 2003 divided by primes less than its square root (which is around 44.7). So, testing primes