, we can remove the absolute values with the appropriate sign. Then, the equation becomes a linear equation in x, and for it to hold for all x in that interval, the coefficients of x and the constants must match on both sides. However, the coefficients and constants would have to be the same across all intervals, which might impose conditions on a, b, c, d.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the only way this equation can hold for all x is if the left-hand side is proportional to the right-hand side's argument inside the absolute value. Let's explore this.\n\nSuppose that |2x + 4| + |ax + b| = |cx + d| for all x. Let's consider specific values of x to generate equations that a, b, c, d must satisfy.\n\nFirst, let's plug in x = -2, which is the critical point of the first absolute value term. At x = -2, the first absolute value becomes |2*(-2) + 4| = | -4 + 4 | = |0| = 0. Then the equation becomes |a*(-2) + b| = |c*(-2) + d|. So:\n\n| -2a + b | = | -2c + d |.\n\nThat's one equation.\n\nNext, let's consider x approaching infinity. For very large x (positive or negative), the behavior of the absolute values will be dominated by the terms with x. Let's analyze the leading coefficients.\n\nFor x \u2192 +\u221e:\n\n|2x + 4| \u2248 2x + 4 (since it's positive)\n|ax + b| \u2248 |ax + b|. If a > 0, then \u2248 ax + b; if a < 0, \u2248 -ax - b.\nSimilarly, |cx + d| \u2248 cx + d if c > 0, else -cx - d.\n\nBut the left-hand side is |2x + 4| + |ax + b|. For very large x, if 2x + 4 and ax + b are both positive, then the left-hand side is (2x + 4) + (ax + b) = (2 + a)x + (4 + b). The right-hand side is |cx + d|. For these to be equal for large x, the coefficients must match. But the right-hand side is either cx + d or -cx - d. However, for x \u2192 +\u221e, if c \u2260 0, then |cx + d| \u2248 |c|x + (d/ c) * sign(c) ). Wait, actually, if c > 0, then |cx + d| = cx + d for large x. If c < 0, then |cx + d| = -cx - d for large x. Similarly, the left-hand side is (2 + a)x + (4 + b) if both ter