ces a_n and b_n where their sum tends to B and product tends to A. Therefore, the necessary and sufficient condition is B\u00b2 - 4A \u2265 0.\n\nWait, but in the case where B\u00b2 - 4A \u2265 0, can we always construct such sequences? If B\u00b2 - 4A \u2265 0, then the quadratic equation x\u00b2 - Bx + A = 0 has real roots, say r1 and r2. Then taking constant sequences a_n = r1 and b_n = r2, we have their sum B and product A. So, constant sequences work. Therefore, all pairs (A, B) with B\u00b2 - 4A \u2265 0 are possible.\n\nBut the question is, are there other pairs where even if B\u00b2 - 4A < 0, you can have non-convergent sequences a_n and b_n such that their sum converges to B and product converges to A? But from the previous attempt, even if we try to make the product go to a value beyond the maximum possible (i.e., when B\u00b2 - 4A < 0), it's not possible. So, perhaps B\u00b2 - 4A \u2265 0 is both necessary and sufficient. Let's verify.\n\nSuppose we have A and B with B\u00b2 - 4A \u2265 0. Then there exist real numbers r and s such that r + s = B and r s = A. Then the constant sequences a_n = r and b_n = s will satisfy lim(a_n + b_n) = B and lim(a_n b_n) = A. So, these pairs are possible.\n\nConversely, suppose there exist sequences a_n and b_n such that lim(a_n + b_n) = B and lim(a_n b_n) = A. We need to show that B\u00b2 - 4A \u2265 0. Assume for contradiction that B\u00b2 - 4A < 0. Then, consider that for large n, a_n + b_n is close to B, and a_n b_n is close to A. Let me define s_n = a_n + b_n and p_n = a_n b_n. Then, the quadratic equation x\u00b2 - s_n x + p_n = 0 has roots a_n and b_n. The discriminant is s_n\u00b2 - 4 p_n. As n \u2192 \u221e, s_n \u2192 B and p_n \u2192 A, so the discriminant tends to B\u00b2 - 4A < 0. Therefore, for sufficiently large n, the discriminant would be negative, implying that a_n and b_n are complex conjugates. But the problem states that the sequences are real, right? The question didn't specify, but since A and B are