he polynomial \\( f(x) \\cap \\mathbb{Z}/p\\mathbb{Z} \\) needs to accommodate this fact.\n\n4. **Minimum Degree \\( d \\):**\n\n   To capture at least \\( p-1 \\) different evaluations (either 0 or 1) due to the nature of the finite field \\( \\mathbb{Z}/p\\mathbb{Z} \\), \\( f(x) \\) must be able to have \\( p-1 \\) terms to match the number of elements. Therefore, the polynomial must have a degree of at least \\( p-1 \\).\n\nWe can conclude that the degree of \\( f(x) \\), denoted as \\( d \\), must satisfy:\n\n\\[\nd \\geqslant p-1\n\\]\n\nThus the proof is complete.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{d \\geqslant p-1}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Find numbers that, when multiplied by 12345679, result in numbers consisting of 9 identical digits.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to find numbers that, when multiplied by 12345679, give a result with nine identical digits. Hmm, let me start by understanding the problem better. The number 12345679 is interesting because it's missing the digit 8. Wait, actually, let me check: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 9. Yeah, it skips 8. I remember hearing something about this number before, maybe related to multiples of 9 or something. \n\nFirst, maybe I should try multiplying 12345679 by some small numbers to see what happens. Let me start with 9. Let's calculate 12345679 \u00d7 9. Let me do the multiplication step by step. 12345679 \u00d7 9: 9\u00d79=81, write down 1 and carry over 8. 9\u00d77=63 +8=71, write down 1, carry 7. 9\u00d76=54 +7=61, write 1, carry 6. 9\u00d75=45 +6=51, write 1, carry 5. 9\u00d74=36 +5=41, write 1, carry 4. 9\u00d73=27 +4=31, write 1, carry 3. 9\u00d72=18 +3=21, write 1, carry 2. 9\u00d71=9 +2=11, write 1, carry 1. Then there's no more digits, so write 1. So the result is 111111111. Oh! So multiplying by 9 gives nine 1s. That's cool. So 9 is one such number.\n\nBut the problem says \"numbers\" plural, so there must be more. Maybe multiples of 