gth of \\( BC \\):\n\nCoordinates of \\( B(a + b, 0) \\) and \\( C(b, a) \\). The distance is:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{(a + b - b)^2 + (0 - a)^2} = \\sqrt{a^2 + a^2} = \\sqrt{2a^2} = a\\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, the length of \\( AD \\) is from \\( A(0,0) \\) to \\( D(a, a) \\):\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{(a - 0)^2 + (a - 0)^2} = \\sqrt{2a^2} = a\\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\nGood, that matches our earlier result. The height is indeed \\( a \\), as the y-coordinate of points \\( C \\) and \\( D \\) is \\( a \\). So this coordinate system works.\n\nNow, to find the circumradius, we can find the circumcircle passing through points \\( A(0,0) \\), \\( B(a + b, 0) \\), \\( C(b, a) \\), and \\( D(a, a) \\). Since it's a cyclic quadrilateral, all four points lie on a circle. To find the radius, we can use the coordinates to determine the circle's equation.\n\nLet's first find the equation of the circle passing through three points \\( A(0,0) \\), \\( B(a + b, 0) \\), and \\( C(b, a) \\). The fourth point \\( D(a, a) \\) should also lie on this circle.\n\nThe general equation of a circle is \\( x^2 + y^2 + Dx + Ey + F = 0 \\). Since point \\( A(0,0) \\) lies on the circle:\n\n\\[\n0 + 0 + 0 + 0 + F = 0 \\implies F = 0\n\\]\n\nSo the equation simplifies to \\( x^2 + y^2 + Dx + Ey = 0 \\).\n\nPoint \\( B(a + b, 0) \\):\n\n\\[\n(a + b)^2 + 0 + D(a + b) + E(0) = 0 \\implies (a + b)^2 + D(a + b) = 0\n\\]\n\\[\nD(a + b) = - (a + b)^2 \\implies D = - (a + b)\n\\]\n\nAssuming \\( a + b \\neq 0 \\), which it isn't since it's the length of the larger base.\n\nNow, point \\( C(b, a) \\):\n\n\\[\nb^2 + a^2 + D b + E a = 0\n\\]\nSubstituting \\( D = - (a + b) \\):\n\n\\[\nb^2 + a^2 - (a + b) b + E a = 0\n\\]\n\\[\nb^2 + a^2 - a b - b^2 + E a = 0\n\\]\nSimplify:\n\n\\[\na^2 - a b + E a = 0 \\implies a(a - b + E) = 0\n\\]\nSince \\( a \\neq 0 \\), we have:\n\n\\[\na - b + E = 0 \\implies E = b - a\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the equation of the circle is:\n\n\\[\nx^2 + y^2 - (a + b)x + (b - a)y = 0\n\\]\n\nTo find the radius, we