d C, then C and D each have degree 2. Now, do they share a common friend? Let's see. C's friends are A and D. D's friends are B and C. So, they don't have any common friends. A is only connected to C, and B is only connected to D. So, the common friends between C and D would be anyone who is friends with both C and D. But the only connection between C and D is each other. So, they don't have a common friend except themselves. Wait, but the problem states that two individuals with the same number of friends do not share any common friends. Since C and D have the same degree (2), they should not share any common friends. However, in this case, they are friends with each other, but they don't have any other common friends. Is being friends with each other considered sharing a common friend? Wait, no. A common friend is a third person who is friends with both. So, if two people are friends, they are directly connected, but unless there's a third person connected to both, they don't share a common friend. So in this case, C and D are friends, but there's no third person who is friends with both C and D. Therefore, they don't share a common friend. So this graph is acceptable.\n\nIn this case, we have edges: A-C, B-D, C-D. That's three edges. But wait, let's check degrees:\n\n- A: degree 1 (connected to C)\n- B: degree 1 (connected to D)\n- C: degree 2 (connected to A and D)\n- D: degree 2 (connected to B and C)\n\nSo, yes, degrees 1,1,2,2. Each pair with the same degree doesn't share any common friends. So, this works, and there are three edges.\n\nBut is this the maximum? Let's see. Suppose we try to add another edge. Let's try connecting A and B. Then, A's degree becomes 2, B's degree becomes 2. C and D are already at degree 2. Now, A and B have the same degree (2), so they shouldn't share any common friends. However, A is connected to C and B, and B is connected to D and A. So, do they share a common friend? A's friends are C and B. B's friends are D and A. So, they are friends with each other but don't share any other friends. Wait, but they are directly connected, so the common friends would be any third person connected to both. But A is connected to C and B, B is connected to D and A. So, they don't have a common friend except each other. But the condition says if two people have the same number of friends, they do not share any common friends. In this case, A and B have degree 2 and are friends, but they don't have a common friend (other than themselves). Wait, but is being friends with each other considered sharing a common friend? No, because a common friend would be a third person. So in this case, they don't share a common friend. Therefore, maybe this is allowed?\n\nWait, but if we connect A and B, then A and B both have degree 2. The condition says if two individuals have the same number of friends, they don't share any common friends. Since they are friends with each other, but there's no third person who is a common friend, so this should be allowed. Then, in this case, the edges would be A-C, B-D, C-D, A-B. That's four edges. Then, degrees:\n\n- A: 2 (C, B)\n- B: 2 (D, A)\n- C: 2 (A, D)\n- D: 2 (B, C)\n\nNow, all four individuals have degree 2. Wait, but the problem states that if two people have the same number of friends, they don't share any common friends. However, in this case, everyone has the same degree (2). So, according to the problem's condition, any two individuals with the same degree (which is everyone) must not share any common friends.\n\nBut in this graph, let's check if any two people share a common friend. Take A and B: A is friends with C and B; B is friends with D and A. Their common friends? Well, they are friends with each other, but do they share a third friend? A is friends with C and B; B is friends with D and A. So, they don't have a common third friend. Similarly, A and C: A is friends with C and B; C is friends with A and D. So, their common friends are... A and C are directly connected