meone. But if the two degree 3 people are connected to everyone, including each other and the two degree 1 people. Then, the two degree 1 people are connected only to the two degree 3 people. Wait, but then each degree 1 person is connected to both degree 3 people. Therefore, the two degree 1 people share two common friends (the degree 3 people). Since they have the same degree, they shouldn't share any common friends. Therefore, this is invalid.\n\nTherefore, that structure doesn't work. So, perhaps the maximum is indeed 3 for n=2.\n\nSo, moving on. Maybe for larger n, there's a pattern. Let's think about generalizing.\n\nThe key seems to be arranging the graph so that people with the same degree don't share common friends. So, if we have multiple degree classes, each class is an independent set, and the neighborhoods of vertices within a class are pairwise disjoint.\n\nThis structure resembles a graph composed of complete bipartite graphs or something similar, but with specific constraints.\n\nWait, another thought: If we have two groups, each of size n, such that everyone in the first group is connected to everyone in the second group. That would be a complete bipartite graph K_{n,n}, which has n^2 edges. However, in this case, all vertices in the same group have the same degree (n). So, for instance, each vertex in the first group has degree n (connected to all n vertices in the second group), and each vertex in the second group also has degree n. But according to the problem's condition, any two vertices with the same degree (i.e., within the same group) cannot share a common friend. However, in K_{n,n}, any two vertices in the same group share all their friends (the entire other group). So, for example, two vertices in the first group share all n friends in the second group. This violates the condition because they have the same degree and share common friends. Therefore, K_{n,n} is invalid.\n\nTherefore, complete bipartite graphs don't work. Hmm.\n\nAnother approach: Let's consider the graph as a union of complete graphs. But again, if we have multiple complete graphs, say multiple triangles or something, but in that case, vertices in the same complete graph have the same degree (if the complete graphs are of the same size), but they share friends, which is not allowed.\n\nAlternatively, maybe a graph where each vertex has a unique set of neighbors, but that might not be possible for large n.\n\nWait, the problem states that if two people have the same number of friends, then they don't share any common friends. So, it's possible for people with different degrees to share common friends. So, the constraint only applies to pairs with the same degree.\n\nTherefore, maybe we can partition the graph into different degree classes where each class is an independent set, and each vertex in a class is connected to a distinct set of vertices in other classes. So that no two vertices in the same class share a neighbor.\n\nThis sounds like a graph composed of a collection of stars. In a star graph, one central node connected to all others, but that's not quite it. Alternatively, if we have multiple stars, where each leaf is connected to a different center. Wait, but in that case, the leaves would have degree 1, and the centers would have higher degrees.\n\nBut if we have multiple stars, the leaves connected to different centers, then the leaves (degree 1) would not share common friends, because each leaf is connected to a different center. Similarly, the centers would have different degrees if the stars are of different sizes. Wait, but if we have multiple centers with the same degree, they would have to not share any common friends.\n\nAlternatively, maybe a graph where each vertex of degree k is connected to a unique set of k vertices, and no two vertices of the same degree share any neighbors.\n\nThis seems similar to a graph with vertices arranged such that same-degree vertices have disjoint neighborhoods. So, the neighborhood