t 99. We want to analyze the form \n    \\[\n    \\frac{P(1) + P(2) + \\ldots + P(n)}{n}.\n    \\]\n    Specifically, since the sum of polynomial evaluations is often considered in telescoping sums, it is insightful to consider a polynomial of the form\n    \\[\n    P(X) = Q(X) - Q(X - 1)\n    \\]\n    where \\( Q(X) \\) is a polynomial with integer coefficients of degree at most 99.\n\n3. **Telescoping Sum**:\n\n    For polynomial \\( P \\) defined as above, the sum \\( \\sum_{i=1}^n (Q(i) - Q(i-1)) \\) telescopes:\n    \\[\n    P(1) + P(2) + \\ldots + P(n) = Q(n) - Q(0).\n    \\]\n    Thus, \n    \\[\n    \\frac{P(1) + P(2) + \\ldots + P(n)}{n} = \\frac{Q(n) - Q(0)}{n}\n    \\]\n    must be an integer.\n\n4. **Choice of Polynomial**:\n\n    We need a polynomial for which \\( Q(n) - Q(0) \\) provides stronger divisibility properties. One effective choice is \n    \\[\n    Q(X) = X^p\n    \\]\n    where \\( p \\) is a prime number.\n\n    Calculate \\( P(X) \\):\n    \\[\n    P(X) = Q(X) - Q(X-1) = X^p - (X-1)^p.\n    \\]\n\n5. **Simpson's Coefficients**:\n\n    By the binomial expansion,\n    \\[\n    P(X) = X^p - (X - 1)^p = \\sum_{i=0}^{p} \\binom{p}{i} X^{p-i} (-1)^i.\n    \\]\n    Simplifying, we get:\n    \\[\n    P(X) = X^p - \\left[ X^p - pX^{p-1} + \\text{lower order terms} \\right],\n    \\]\n    leading to \n    \\[\n    P(X) = pX^{p-1} - \\text{lower order terms}.\n    \\]\n\n6. **Reduced Polynomial**:\n\n    We aim to make most coefficients divisible by \\( p \\). Consider:\n    \\[\n    \\frac{Q(X) - Q(X-1)}{p} = \\frac{X^p - (X-1)^p}{p}.\n    \\]\n    Because \\( \\binom{p}{k} \\) is divisible by \\( p \\) for \\( 1 \\leq k \\leq p-1 \\) (by Lucas' theorem on binomial coefficients and prime divisors),\n    \\[\n    \\frac{X^p - (X-1)^p - 1}{p}\n    \\]\n    is a polynomial with integer coefficients.\n\n7. **Applying Hypothesis**:\n\n    Given that \\( R(X) = \\frac{X^p - (X-1)^p - 1}{p} \\) is an integer-coefficient polynomial, we apply the initial hypothesis:\n    \\[\n    \\frac{R(1) + R(2) + \\ldots + R(n)}{n}.\n    \\]\n    Telescope the sum \\( Q(X - 1) \\):\n    \\[\n    \\sum_{i=1}^n R(i-1) \\text{ gives } R(n-1) - R(0),\n    \\]\n    and:\n    \\[\n    \\frac{Q(n) - Q(0) - n}{np} = \\frac{n^p - n}{np}\n    \\]\n    must be an integer.\n\n8. **Contradiction Proof**:\n\n    If \\( p \\) divides \\( n \\), both \\( np \\) divides \\( n \\) by requirement, simplifying:\n    \\[\n    \\frac{n^p - n}{np} \\simeq \\frac{n^p}{np} - \\frac{n}{np} = n^{p-1} - \\frac{1}{p},\n    \\]\n    showing a contradiction unless \\( n \\) is not divisible by any prime \\( p \\) in \\( \\{2, ..., 99\\} \\).\n\n### Conclusion:\n    Therefore, \\( n \\) cannot have any prime divisors between 2 and 100:\n    \\[\n    \\boxed{\\text{n does not have any divisors between 2 and 100.}}\n    \\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Let \\(ABC\\) be a triangle with circumcircle \\(\\Gamma\\) and incenter \\(I\\). Let \\(M\\) be the midpoint of side \\(BC\\). Denote by \\(D\\) the foot of the perpendicular from \\(I\\) to side \\(BC\\). The line through \\(I\\) perpendicular to \\(AI\\) meets sides \\(AB\\) and \\(AC\\) at \\(F\\) and \\(E\\) respectively. Suppose the circumcircle of triangle \\(AEF\\) intersects \\(\\Gamma\\) at a point \\(X\\) other than \\(A\\). Prove that lines \\(XD\\) and \\(AM\\) meet on \\(\\Gamma\\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to prove that lines XD and AM meet on \u0393. Let me try to break this problem down step by step. First, let me recall all the given information and try to visualize the triangle and the various points mentioned.\n\nWe have triangle ABC with circumcircle \u0393 and incenter I. M is the midpoint of BC. D is the foot of the perpendicular from I to BC. Then there's a line through I perpendicular to AI, which meets AB at F and AC at E. The circumcircle of triangle AEF intersects \u0393 again at point X (other than A)