rom cities in T must stay within T (since S cannot reach T).\n     - Total edges from T: \\(1 + 20(|T| - 1)\\) (Dalniy's 1 edge + 20 edges per other city in T).\n\n5. **Inequalities for Subgraphs**:\n   - **For S**:\n     \\[\n     21 + 20(|S| - 1) \\leq |S|(|S| - 1) \\implies |S|^2 - 21|S| - 1 \\geq 0 \\implies |S| \\geq 22.\n     \\]\n   - **For T**:\n     \\[\n     1 + 20(|T| - 1) \\leq |T|(|T| - 1) \\implies |T|^2 - 21|T| + 19 \\geq 0 \\implies |T| \\geq 21.\n     \\]\n\n6. **Total Cities Contradiction**:\n   - Total cities \\(N = |S| + |T| \\geq 22 + 21 = 43\\).\n   - However, the **actual minimum number of cities** is determined by:\n     - Capital's 21 outgoing edges \u2192 21 distinct cities directly connected.\n     - Including the capital and Dalniy: \\(N \\geq 23\\).\n   - This contradicts \\(N \\geq 43\\), proving the assumption false.\n\n**Conclusion**: The assumption that no path exists leads to an impossible number of cities. Therefore, there **must** be a path from the capital to Dalniy.", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Setup and Assumptions:**\n   - We consider a graph where each vertex represents a city, and each edge represents a possible flight (route) between two cities.\n   - We will focus on the connected component of this graph that includes the capital.\n\n2. **Component Analysis:**\n   - Let us assume that the connected component containing the capital does not include the city of Dalniy.\n\n3. **Degree of Vertices:**\n   - In this connected component, the vertex corresponding to the capital has degree 21, as there are 21 flights (routes) starting from the capital.\n   - All other cities in this connected component have a degree of 20 since each other city has 20 routes available.\n\n4. **Graph Theory Argument:**\n   - We use the Handshaking Lemma, which states that in any graph, the sum of the degrees of all vertices is even since each edge contributes 2 to the total degree count.\n   - Calculating the sum of degrees in the component:\n 