MNP.\n\nCoordinates:\n\nM: (r, 0)\n\nN: (-r, 0)\n\nP: ( r*sin(\u03b3/2), r*cos(\u03b3/2) )\n\nUsing the shoelace formula:\n\nArea = (1/2)| (x_M(y_N - y_P) + x_N(y_P - y_M) + x_P(y_M - y_N) ) |\n\nPlugging in the coordinates:\n\nx_M = r, y_M = 0\n\nx_N = -r, y_N = 0\n\nx_P = r*sin(\u03b3/2), y_P = r*cos(\u03b3/2)\n\nCompute each term:\n\nFirst term: x_M(y_N - y_P) = r*(0 - r*cos(\u03b3/2)) = -r\u00b2 cos(\u03b3/2)\n\nSecond term: x_N(y_P - y_M) = (-r)*(r*cos(\u03b3/2) - 0) = -r\u00b2 cos(\u03b3/2)\n\nThird term: x_P(y_M - y_N) = r*sin(\u03b3/2)*(0 - 0) = 0\n\nSumming these: -r\u00b2 cos(\u03b3/2) - r\u00b2 cos(\u03b3/2) + 0 = -2 r\u00b2 cos(\u03b3/2)\n\nTaking absolute value and multiplying by 1/2:\n\nArea of MNP = (1/2)| -2 r\u00b2 cos(\u03b3/2) | = (1/2)(2 r\u00b2 cos(\u03b3/2)) = r\u00b2 cos(\u03b3/2)\n\nSimilarly, compute the area of triangle MQN.\n\nCoordinates:\n\nM: (r, 0)\n\nQ: ( r*sin(\u03b1/2), r*cos(\u03b1/2) )\n\nN: (-r, 0)\n\nShoelace formula:\n\nArea = (1/2)| x_M(y_Q - y_N) + x_Q(y_N - y_M) + x_N(y_M - y_Q) |\n\nPlugging in:\n\nx_M = r, y_M = 0\n\nx_Q = r*sin(\u03b1/2), y_Q = r*cos(\u03b1/2)\n\nx_N = -r, y_N = 0\n\nCompute each term:\n\nFirst term: x_M(y_Q - y_N) = r*(r*cos(\u03b1/2) - 0) = r\u00b2 cos(\u03b1/2)\n\nSecond term: x_Q(y_N - y_M) = r*sin(\u03b1/2)*(0 - 0) = 0\n\nThird term: x_N(y_M - y_Q) = (-r)*(0 - r*cos(\u03b1/2)) = (-r)*(-r cos(\u03b1/2)) = r\u00b2 cos(\u03b1/2)\n\nSumming these: r\u00b2 cos(\u03b1/2) + 0 + r\u00b2 cos(\u03b1/2) = 2 r\u00b2 cos(\u03b1/2)\n\nTaking absolute value and multiplying by 1/2:\n\nArea of MQN = (1/2)(2 r\u00b2 cos(\u03b1/2)) = r\u00b2 cos(\u03b1/2)\n\nTherefore, the ratio of the areas of triangles MNP and MQN is:\n\nArea(MNP)/Area(MQN) = [r\u00b2 cos(\u03b3/2)] / [r\u00b2 cos(\u03b1/2)] = cos(\u03b3/2) / cos(\u03b1/2)\n\nSo, the ratio is cos(\u03b3/2) : cos(\u03b1/2), or written as a fraction, cos(\u03b3/2) / cos(\u03b1/2).\n\nTherefore, the final answer is cos(\u03b3/2) divided by cos(\u03b1/2), which can be written as \\boxed{\\dfrac{\\cos \\frac{\\gamma}{2}}{\\cos \\frac{\\alpha}{2}}}.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe ratio of the areas of triangles \\(MNP\\) and \\(MQN\\) is \\boxed{\\dfrac{\\cos \\frac{\\gamma}{2}}{\\cos \\frac{\\alpha}{2}}}.", "deepseek_solution": "To find the ratio of the areas of triangles \\(MNP\\) and \\(MQN\\) in triangle \\(ABC\\) with an inscribed circle centered at \\(O\\), given angles \\(\\angle A = \\alpha\\) and \\(\\angle C = \\gamma\\), we proceed as follows:\n\n1. **Identify Key Points and Coordinates**:\n   - The incenter \\(O\\) is the center of the incircle with radius \\(r\\).\n   - Points \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) are where line \\(BO\\) intersects the incircle, diametrically opposite each other on the x-axis: \\(M(r, 0)\\) and \\(N(-r, 0)\\).\n   - Points \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) are where segments \\(AO\\) and \\(CO\\) intersect the incircle, respectively. Their coordinates are determined using trigonometric identities:\n     - \\(P(r \\sin(\\gamma/2), r \\cos(\\gamma/2))\\)\n     - \\(Q(r \\sin(\\alpha/2), r \\cos(\\alpha/2))\\)\n\n2. **Calculate Areas Using Shoelace Formula**:\n   - **Area of Triangle \\(MNP\\)**:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Area} = \\frac{1}{2} \\left| r(0 - r \\cos(\\gamma/2)) + (-r)(r \\cos(\\gamma/2) - 0) + r \\sin(\\gamma/2)(0 - 0) \\right| = r^2 \\cos(\\gamma/2)\n     \\]\n   - **Area of Triangle \\(MQN\\)**:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Area} = \\frac{1}{2} \\left| r(r \\cos(\\alpha/2) - 0) + r \\sin(\\alpha/2)(0 - 0) + (-r)(0 - r \\cos(\\alpha/2)) \\right| = r^2 \\cos(\\alpha/2)\n     \\]\n\n3. **Determine the Ratio**:\n   - The ratio of the areas is:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{\\text{Area of } \\triangle MNP}{\\text{Area of } \\triangle MQN} = \\frac{r^2 \\cos(\\gamma/2)}{r^2 \\cos(\\alpha/2)} = \\frac{\\cos(\\gamma/2)}{\\cos(\\alpha/2)}\n     \\]\n\nThus, the ratio of the areas of triangles \\(MNP\\) and \\(MQN\\) is \\(\\boxed{\\dfrac{\\cos \\frac{\\gamma}{2}}{\\cos \\frac{\\alpha}{2}}}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "1. Let \\( R \\) be the radius of the inscribed circle of triangle \\( ABC \\). The line \\( BO \\), being a radius intersecting the circle, also serves as an angle bisector of \\( \\angle ABC \\).\n\n2. The projections of points \\( P \\) and \\( Q \\) onto the line \\( BO \\) are denoted as \\( K \\) and \\( F \\), respectively. We need to determine the distances from these points to line \\( BO \\).\n\n3. Calculate the angle \\( \\angle POK \\):\n    \\[\n    \\angle POK = \\angle BAO + \\angle ABD\n    \\]\n    Here, \\( \\angle BAO \\) is half of \\( \\angle A \\), and \\( \\angle ABD \\) is half of the remaining angle \\( \\pi - \\angle A - \\angle C \\):\n    \\[\n    \\angle BAO = \\frac{\\alpha}{2}, \\quad \\angle ABD = \\frac{\\pi - \\alpha - \\gamma}{2}\n    \\]\n    Therefore:\n    \\[\n    \\angle POK = \\frac{\\alpha}{2} + \\frac{\\pi - \\alpha - \\gamma}{2} = \\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\frac{\\gamma}{2}\n    \\]\n\n4. Using trigonometric identities, we can write:\n    \\[\n    PK = PO \\sin (\\angle POK) = PO \\sin \\left( \\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\frac{\\gamma}{2} \\right)\n    \\]\n    Since \\( PO = R \\) (the radius of the circle), we get:\n    \\[\n    PK = R \\cos \\frac{\\gamma}{2}\n    \\]\n\n5. Similarly, for point \\( Q \\):\n    \\[\n    \\angle QOF = \\angle CAO + \\angle ABD = \\frac{\\gamma}{2} + \\frac{\\pi - \\alpha - \\gamma}{2} = \\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\frac{\\alpha}{2}\n    \\]\n    And:\n    \\[\n    QF = QO \\sin (\\angle QOF) = QO \\sin \\left( \\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\frac{\\alpha}{2} \\right) = R \\cos \\frac{\\alpha}{2}\n    \\]\n\n6. To find the ratio of the areas of triangles \\( MNP \\) and \\( MNQ \\):\n    \\[\n    \\frac{S_{\\triangle MNP}}{S_{\\triangle MNQ}} = \\frac{PK}{QF} = \\frac{R \\cos \\frac{\\gamma}{2}}{R \\cos \\frac{\\alpha}{2}} = \\frac{\\cos \\frac{\\gamma}{2}}{\\cos \\frac{\\alpha}{2}}\n    \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{\\cos \\frac{\\gamma}{2}}{\\cos \\frac{\\alpha}{2}}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_c