on \\( 4k^4 - 8k^2 + 2 \\) yields positive integers. Each distinct integer \\( k \\) results in a distinct \\( N \\), demonstrating there are infinitely many such \\( N \\).\n\nThus, the polynomial \\( x^8 + Nx^4 + 1 \\) factors into two quartic polynomials with integer coefficients for infinitely many natural numbers \\( N \\) given by the parameterization:\n\\[\n\\boxed{4k^4 - 8k^2 + 2}\n\\]\nwhere \\( k \\) is an integer.", "ground_truth_solution": "To prove the existence of infinitely many natural numbers $N$ such that the polynomial \\(x^{8} + N x^{4} + 1\\) can be factored into two polynomials of degree four with integer coefficients, we shall show that such a factorization exists for a specific form of \\(N\\).\n\n1. Consider \\(N = 4k^{4} - 8k^{2} + 2\\) for some integer \\(k\\).\n   \n2. Substituting this value of \\(N\\) into the polynomial, we get:\n   \\[\n   x^8 + \\left(4k^4 - 8k^2 + 2\\right)x^4 + 1\n   \\]\n\n3. To factorize the polynomial \\(x^8 + (4k^4 - 8k^2 + 2)x^4 + 1\\), we attempt to write it as the product of two fourth-degree polynomials:\n   \\[\n   \\left(x^4 - 2kx^3 + 2k^2x^2 - 2kx + 1\\right)\\left(x^4 + 2kx^3 + 2k^2x^2 + 2kx + 1\\right)\n   \\]\n\n4. Let's expand this product to verify our factorization:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &\\left(x^4 - 2k x^3 + 2k^2 x^2 - 2k x + 1\\right) \\left(x^4 + 2k x^3 + 2k^2 x^2 + 2k x + 1\\right) \\\\\n   &= x^4 (x^4 + 2k x^3 + 2k^2 x^2 + 2k x + 1) \\\\\n   &\\quad - 2k x^3 (x^4 + 2k x^3 + 2k^2 x^2 + 2k x + 1) \\\\\n   &\\quad + 2k^2 x^2 (x^4 + 2k x^3 + 2k^2 x^2 + 2k x + 1) \\\\\n   &\\quad - 2k x (x^4 + 2k x^3 + 2k^2 x^2 + 2k x + 1) \\\\\n   &\\quad + 1 (x^4 + 2k x^3 + 2k^2 x^2 + 2k x + 1)\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n5. Combining the terms, we get:\n   \\[\n   x^8 + 2kx^7 + 2k^2x^6 + 2kx^5 + x^4 - 2kx^7 - 4k^2x^6 - 4k^3x^5 - 2kx^4 - 2kx^3 + 2k^2x^6 + 4k^4x^4 + 4k^2x^2 - 2k^3x^5 - 4kx^4 - 4kx^3 + 2kx^5 + 4kx^4 + 2kx^3 + 1 = x^8 + (4k^4 - 8k^2 + 2)x^4 + 1\n   \\]\n\n6. The middle terms simplify a