re $AB > BC$.\n\n2. Place a point $C_1$ on the side $AB$ such that $BC_1 = BC$.\n\n3. By the properties of the angle bisector, if $BD$ is the angle bisector, then $AD = DC$.\n\n4. Since $BD$ is the angle bisector, it divides the opposite side $AC$ into segments proportionate to the other two sides $AB$ and $BC$. Hence, we have the Angle Bisector Theorem:\n    \\[\n    \\frac{AD}{DC} = \\frac{AB}{BC}\n    \\]\n\n5. Given $AB > BC$, imply $AD > DC$. Let us consider the symmetric point $C_1$ with respect to $BD$. According to the Angle Bisector Theorem:\n    \\[\n    \\frac{AD}{DC} = \\frac{AB}{BC}\n    \\]\n    So, since \\(AD > DC\\), this implies that the segment $AC$ is divided into $AD + DC$ such that $AD > DC$.\n\n6. Construct an external point $C_1$ symmetric to $C$ about the bisector $BD$ such that $BC_1 = BC$. Therefore, reflective symmetry about the bisector guarantees:\n    \\[\n    CD = C_1 D \n    \\]\n\n7. Since $BC_1 = BC < AB$, it means the point $C_1$ exists on the segment $AB$, creating an extended angle configuration, where:\n   \\[\n   \\angle AC_1D > \\angle BDC \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\angle BDC = \\angle BDC_1 > \\angle A\n   \\]\n\n8. Therefore:\n    \\[\n    CD = C_1 D < AD\n    \\]\n\n9. Hence, it follows from the properties of the angle bisector of a triangle, which divides the opposite side into segments proportional to the other two sides.\n\n10. Therefore, we have established that the segment adjacent to the larger side $AB$ is proportional and divides based on the length of those sides, such that the line segment $AD > CD$ respectively showing the proportional relationship based on $AB > BC$.\n\n#### Conclusion:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The side corresponding to the larger segment adjacent to the greater side of the triangle's angle bisector is always greater.}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "In triangle \\( \\triangle ABC \\), it is given that \\( \\angle C=90^\\