d{3}\n   \\]\n\n7. The multiplicative inverse of 2 modulo 3 is:\n   \\[\n   2 \\times 2 = 4 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{3} \\implies 2^{-1} \\equiv 2 \\pmod{3}\n   \\]\n\n8. Therefore:\n   \\[\n   b \\equiv 2 \\pmod{3} \\implies b = 3k + 2 \\quad \\text{for integer} \\ k\n   \\]\n\n9. Substitute back to find the maximum value:\n   \\[\n   a + b = \\frac{115 - 5(3k + 2)}{3} + (3k + 2)\n   \\]\n\n   Simplify the expression:\n   \\[\n   a + b = \\frac{115 - 15k - 10}{3} + 3k + 2 = \\frac{105 - 15k}{3} + 3k + 2 = 35 - 5k + 3k + 2 = 37 - 2k\n   \\]\n\n10. To maximize \\(a + b\\):\n    - \\(k\\) should be as small as possible (since \\(b \\geq 0\\), \\(k \\geq 0\\)).\n    - If \\(k = 0\\): \\(b = 2\\), then:\n\n11. Check:\n    \\[\n    a = \\frac{115 - 5(2)}{3} = \\frac{115 - 10}{3} = 35\n    \\]\n    \\[\n    a + b = 35 + 2 = 37\n    \\]\n\n12. So, the maximum possible sum of the number of people in both classes is 37.\n\nConclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{37}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Let \\( n \\) be an integer greater than 3. Let \\( R \\) be the set of lattice points \\( (x, y) \\) such that \\( 0 \\leq x, y \\leq n \\) and \\( |x-y| \\leq 3 \\). Let \\( A_n \\) be the number of paths from \\( (0,0) \\) to \\( (n,n) \\) that consist only of steps of the form \\( (x, y) \\rightarrow (x, y+1) \\) and \\( (x, y) \\rightarrow (x+1, y) \\) and are contained entirely within \\( R \\). Find the smallest positive real number that is greater than \\( \\frac{A_{n+1}}{A_n} \\) for all \\( n \\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to find the smallest positive real number that is greater than \\( \\frac{A_{n+1}}{A_n} \\) for all \\( n > 3 \\). Here, \\( A_n \\) is the number of paths from (0,0) to (n,n) using only right and up steps, and staying within the set \\( R \\), which consists of lattice points where \\( 0 \\leq x, y \\leq n \\) and \\( |x - y| \\leq 3 \\).\n\nFirst, let me understand the problem. The set