nTotal time until the second meeting.\n\nFrom start to first meeting: t1 = 300 / v1 = (D - 300) / v2\n\nFrom first meeting to reaching the end:\n\nFor Athlete 1: time_a1 = (D - 300) / v1\n\nFor Athlete 2: time_a2 = 300 / v2\n\nThen, after reaching the ends, they turn around and start running back. Let the time from when they turn around until they meet again be t2.\n\nDuring t2, Athlete 1 runs distance: v1 * t2\n\nAthlete 2 runs distance: v2 * t2\n\nAt the time of the second meeting, the total distance covered by Athlete 1 is 300 + (D - 300) + v1 * t2 = D + v1 * t2\n\nSimilarly, Athlete 2 has covered (D - 300) + 300 + v2 * t2 = D + v2 * t2\n\nBut the meeting point is 400 m from B. So, from B, Athlete 1 has run v1 * t2 = 400 m. Therefore, v1 * t2 = 400 => t2 = 400 / v1\n\nSimilarly, from A, Athlete 2 has run v2 * t2. But the meeting point is D - 400 m from A. Wait, since it's 400 m from B, which is D - 400 m from A. So, Athlete 2, starting from A, runs D - 400 m to reach the meeting point. Therefore, v2 * t2 = D - 400\n\nBut we already have t2 = 400 / v1, so substituting into the second equation:\n\nv2 * (400 / v1) = D - 400\n\n=> (v2 / v1) * 400 = D - 400\n\nFrom equation (1) earlier, we have v1 / v2 = 300 / (D - 300), which implies v2 / v1 = (D - 300) / 300\n\nTherefore, substituting into the above equation:\n\n[(D - 300) / 300] * 400 = D - 400\n\nSimplify:\n\n[(D - 300) * 400] / 300 = D - 400\n\nMultiply both sides by 300:\n\n(D - 300) * 400 = 300 * (D - 400)\n\nLet's compute both sides:\n\nLeft: 400D - 120,000\n\nRight: 300D - 120,000\n\nSet equal:\n\n400D - 120,000 = 300D - 120,000\n\nWait, subtract 300D both sides:\n\n100D - 120,000 = -120,000\n\nAdd 120,000 to both sides:\n\n100D = 0\n\nWhich gives D = 0. That can't be right. Hmm, that's a problem. That suggests a contradiction, which means I made a mistake in my reasoning.\n\nWait, let's check the steps again.\n\nSo, we had from the first meeting:\n\nv1 / v2 = 300 / (D - 300) ...(1)\n\nThen, after they reach 