stance D - 300 - 400 = D - 700? Wait, no. Wait, if the second meeting is 400 m from B, then from B, Athlete 1 has run 400 m towards A. So total distance from first meeting to second meeting for Athlete 1 is (D - 300) + 400.\n\nSimilarly, Athlete 2 goes from meeting point (D - 300 from B) to A (300 m), then back to 400 m from B, which is D - 400 m from A. So from first meeting to second meeting, Athlete 2 runs 300 + (D - 400).\n\nTherefore, total distance covered by both from first meeting to second meeting is [(D - 300) + 400] + [300 + (D - 400)] = (D + 100) + (D - 100) = 2D\n\nTherefore, total distance covered by both by second meeting is D (first meeting) + 2D (afterwards) = 3D.\n\nSince they are running at constant speeds, the ratio of their speeds should be the same as before.\n\nAt the first meeting, the ratio of distances is 300 : (D - 300) = v1 : v2\n\nAt the second meeting, the total distances covered by each would be:\n\nAthlete 1: 300 + (D - 300) + 400 = D + 400\n\nAthlete 2: (D - 300) + 300 + (D - 400) = 2D - 400 - 300 + 300? Wait, let's recalculate.\n\nWait, Athlete 2 starts at B, runs D - 300 to first meeting, then 300 to A, then D - 400 to the second meeting. So total distance: (D - 300) + 300 + (D - 400) = D - 300 + 300 + D - 400 = 2D - 400.\n\nSimilarly, Athlete 1's total distance is 300 + (D - 300) + 400 = D + 400.\n\nSince they started at the same time and ran until the second meeting, the total time is the same for both. Therefore, the ratio of their speeds is equal to the ratio of the total distances:\n\nv1 / v2 = (D + 400) / (2D - 400)\n\nBut from the first meeting, we also have v1 / v2 = 300 / (D - 300)\n\nTherefore:\n\n300 / (D - 300) = (D + 400) / (2D - 400)\n\nCross-multiplying:\n\n300*(2D - 400) = (D + 400)*(D - 300)\n\nCompute left side: 600D - 120,000\n\nRight side: D^2 - 300D + 400D - 120,000 = D^2 + 100D - 120,000\n\nSet equal:\n\n600D - 120,000 = D^2 + 100D - 120,000\n\nSubtract 600D - 120,000 from both sides:\n\n0 = D^2 + 100D - 120,000 - 600D + 120,000\n\nSimplify:\n\n0 = D^2 - 500D\n\nSo, D^2 - 500D = 0\n\nFactor:\n\nD(D - 500) = 0\n\nSolutions: D = 0 or D = 500\n\nSince D can't be 0, D = 500 meters.\n\nLet me check this. If AB is 500 meters.\n\nFirst meeting at 300 m from A, so 200 m from B.\n\nSpeeds ratio: v1/v2 = 300/200 = 3/2. So Athlete 1 is faster.\n\nTotal distance by second meeting:\n\nAthlete 1: 300 + (500 - 300) + 400 = 300 + 200 + 400 = 900 m\n\nAthlete 2: (500 - 300) + 300 + (500 - 400) = 200 + 300 + 100 = 600 m\n\nRatio of distances 900:600 = 3:2, which matches the speed ratio. So it's consistent.\n\nAlso, total time until second meeting:\n\nFor Athlete 1: 900 / v1\n\nFor Athlete 2: 600 / v2\n\nSince v1 = (3/2)v2, 900 / ( (3/2)v2 ) = (900 * 2) / (3v2 ) = 600 / v2, which equals Athlete 2's time. So that checks out.\n\nAnother check: time to reach the ends after first meeting.\n\nAthlete 1: 200 m to B at speed v1. Since v1 = 3/2 v2, time = 200 / (3/2 v2 ) = 400 / (3 v2 )\n\nAthlete 2: 300 m to A at speed v2. Time = 300 / v2\n\nSo Athlete 1 takes 400/(3v2) \u2248 133.33 / v2 time, Athlete 2 takes 300 / v2 time. So Athlete 1 arrives at B first, turns around, and starts running back. Athlete 2 arrives at A later, turns around, and starts running back.\n\nThe time between Athlete 1 turning around and Athlete 2 turning around is 300 / v2 - 400 / (3v2 ) = (900 - 400)/ (3v2 ) = 500 / (3v2 )\n\nDuring that time, Athlete 1 is running back towards A at speed v1 = 3/2 v2. So distance covered: (3/2 v2 ) * (500 / (3v2 )) = (3/2)*(500/3) = 250 meters. So when Athlete 2 starts running back, Athlete 1 is already 250 meters from B towards A.\n\nAt that point, the distance between them is 500 - 250 = 250 meters. Now, both are running towards each other: Athlete 1 at speed v1 = 3/2 v2 towards A, and Athlete 2 at speed v2 towards B. Wait, no. Wait, when Athlete 2 starts running back towards B, Athlete 1 is 250 meters from B towards A, so the distance between them is 250 meters. But Athlete 1 is moving